window.__hvBdRegister("seneca-tragedies", {"key":"seneca-tragedies","sections":[{"i":0,"j":23,"loc":"Introduction; The Madness of Hercules, lines 1–201","title":"Juno's prologue and the dawn chorus","summary":"After a short note on the ten tragedies and Seneca's birth at Cordoba, The Madness of Hercules opens with its cast list and argument. Juno, driven from heaven by Jove's mistresses and their stars, complains that every labour she sets Hercules only adds to his glory; now he has broken into the underworld and brought up Cerberus, and she fears he will storm heaven itself. Since no monster can match him, she resolves to set him against himself: she summons Discord, Crime, Impiety and Madness and the Furies to drive him mad, so that he returns to find his sons unharmed and then kills them. The Theban chorus greets the dawn, contrasts the calm work of shepherds and fishermen with the anxious ambition of cities, warns that life is short and the Fates do not wait, and prefers a humble, safe life to glory. It ends by announcing Megara, her children and Amphitryon.","ideas":["Juno's hatred has only proved Hercules his father's son","Her plan: since Hercules has no equal, let him war with himself","The chorus praises the quiet country life over city ambition","No one can delay the day the Fates have set"],"terms":[{"t":"Alcides","d":"Name for Hercules as grandson of Alcaeus, used throughout the play."},{"t":"Eumenides / Furies","d":"Underworld avenging goddesses whom Juno calls up to madden Hercules."}]},{"i":24,"j":84,"loc":"lines 202–523","title":"Lycus demands Megara at the altar","summary":"Amphitryon prays for an end to the family's troubles, recounts Hercules' labours from the strangled serpents to the Augean stables, and laments that Thebes, with its king Creon and his sons murdered, now lies under the usurper Lycus. Megara prays for her husband's return or to follow him in death. Lycus enters, boasting that he has valour rather than ancestry, and plans to secure his rule by marrying Megara, offering her peace; she refuses, saying her hatred of him is all she has left. In sharp exchanges Lycus mocks Hercules as a slave who wore a woman's robes, while Amphitryon defends his son's divine birth and deeds. When Megara swears she will be a Danaid to him, Lycus orders the suppliants burned in the temple and leaves to sacrifice to Neptune; then the earth rumbles, and Amphitryon hears Hercules returning.","ideas":["Without Hercules, crime succeeds and is called virtue","Lycus: the first art of kings is to bear hatred","Megara: one who can be forced has not learned to die","The quarrel over whether Hercules is son of Jove or a slave"],"terms":[{"t":"Danaïdes","d":"Daughters of Danaus who killed their husbands on their wedding night; Megara threatens to be one more."}]},{"i":85,"j":101,"loc":"lines 524–657","title":"Chorus on Fortune; Hercules returns","summary":"The chorus complains that Fortune is unjust to the good, letting Eurystheus rest while Hercules labours, and recalls his journeys to Scythia and the Amazons and down to Proserpina's realm; Orpheus overcame the underworld by song, so strength can overcome it too. Hercules enters with Theseus, asks the Sun and gods to forgive him for bringing hidden things to light, and asks Juno what she will order next. Seeing soldiers at the shrine and his family in mourning, he learns from Amphitryon that Lycus has killed Creon and means to kill them all. He hurries off to kill Lycus, leaving Theseus to guard them. Theseus assures Megara that Lycus is as good as dead, and Amphitryon asks him to tell of the descent to the underworld, which Theseus is reluctant to recall.","ideas":["Fortune favours the idle and burdens the brave","Orpheus as proof that the underworld can be overcome","Hercules' first words are a demand for his next task","His return is immediately turned toward vengeance on Lycus"],"terms":[{"t":"Dis","d":"Roman name for Pluto, king of the underworld."}]},{"i":102,"j":121,"loc":"lines 658–894","title":"Theseus describes the underworld","summary":"Theseus tells how the way down opens at Taenarus in a dim half-light, with Lethe winding through it, Cocytus, and personified Sleep, Hunger, Fear, Grief, Disease, War and Age; the land below is barren and worse than death. He describes the palace of Dis, the judges Minos, Rhadamanthus and Aeacus, the rule that each suffers what he has done, and the warning that rulers should abstain from human blood; then the punishments of Ixion, Sisyphus, Tantalus, Tityos and the Danaids. Hercules forced Charon's boat, beat Cerberus into submission with his club behind the lion skin, chained him, and with Theseus' help dragged him into daylight, where the dog hid from the sun. The chorus compares the crowds of the dead to festival crowds, prays that old age bring them there late, and celebrates the peace Hercules has won for Thebes and the world.","ideas":["The underworld as a barren, motionless, joyless land","Judgement in which the crime returns upon its author; mild rulers are rewarded","The taming and dragging up of Cerberus","The chorus: all are hastening toward death; rejoice in Hercules' peace"],"terms":[{"t":"Taenarus","d":"Spartan headland where an entrance to the underworld was believed to lie."},{"t":"Cerberus","d":"Three-headed dog guarding the realm of Dis, brought up by Hercules as his last labour."}]},{"i":122,"j":156,"loc":"lines 895–1137","title":"Hercules' madness and the killings","summary":"Hercules returns having killed Lycus and prepares sacrifices, praying for universal peace and that any monster still to come be his to fight. Juno's madness seizes him: he sees night at midday and the Nemean lion raging among the stars, plans to storm heaven with the Titans, and sees Giants and Furies. Taking his own sons for Lycus' children, he shoots one, dashes another against the stones, and kills Megara and her infant with his club, believing he has caught Juno; Amphitryon offers himself as the last victim. Hercules then collapses into deep sleep and his weapons are removed. The chorus mourns, calls on Sleep to calm him, reflects that only madness can now keep him innocent, and bids the dead boys go to the shades.","ideas":["Hercules' prayer for peace turns into delusion","He kills wife and sons believing them his enemies","Amphitryon's offer of himself","Chorus: next best to guiltless hands is ignorance of guilt"],"terms":[{"t":"furor (madness)","d":"The frenzy Juno sends, which turns Hercules' strength against his own house."}]},{"i":157,"j":200,"loc":"lines 1138–1344","title":"Hercules wakes and chooses to live","summary":"Hercules wakes in his right mind, sees the bodies and demands to know who has done it, while Amphitryon and Theseus turn away in silence. Recognizing his own Hydra-poisoned arrows, he understands; Amphitryon says the woe is his but the crime Juno's. Hercules calls for punishment, a pyre, Prometheus' crags or the depths of Tartarus, and vows to burn his weapons; when Theseus urges him to bear it, he insists that death alone can heal his sin and threatens to pull Thebes down on himself. Amphitryon returns his arms but sets a sword to his own breast, saying Hercules' death will be his own. Hercules yields, adds living to his labours, asks where he can be cleansed, and Theseus offers Athens, where Mars once cleansed his hands.","ideas":["Recognition through his own arrows","Debate over error versus guilt","Amphitryon's threat of suicide forces Hercules to live","Exile and purification offered by Theseus in Athens"],"terms":[{"t":"error vs. scelus","d":"Amphitryon and Theseus distinguish mistake from crime; Hercules replies that great error often takes the place of guilt."}]},{"i":201,"j":221,"loc":"The Trojan Women: introduction; lines 1–163","title":"Hecuba and the chorus mourn Troy","summary":"After the cast and argument (the Greeks wait to sail; Achilles' ghost demands Polyxena and Calchas demands Astyanax), Hecuba opens the play among the smoking ruins, offering Troy as proof of how frail power is. She claims she foresaw the disaster before Cassandra, describes Pyrrhus killing Priam at the altar, and notes that the captive women are about to be allotted to Greek masters. She leads the chorus of Trojan women in a formal lament: they bare their shoulders and beat their breasts, first for Hector, the wall of Troy, and then for Priam. The section ends with the refrain that Priam is happy, since in death he escapes captivity and the triumph of the Greeks.","ideas":["Troy as an example of the instability of fortune","The murder of Priam at the altar","Ritual mourning for Hector, then Priam","Happy Priam: death frees him from slavery"],"terms":[{"t":"Pergamum / Ilium","d":"Names for Troy and its citadel."}]},{"i":222,"j":262,"loc":"lines 164–370","title":"Achilles' demand and the quarrel of chiefs","summary":"Talthybius reports that an earthquake opened Achilles' tomb and his ghost demanded that Polyxena be sacrificed on it by Pyrrhus before the fleet may sail. Pyrrhus claims this due, listing his father's conquests and citing Agamemnon's own sacrifice of Iphigenia. Agamemnon answers that the victor should be moderate, that Troy's fall teaches how quickly greatness falls, that he regrets the night's excesses, and that human sacrifice to the dead is not honour; he will not let murder be called marriage. The two exchange insults about Priam's death, Achilles' lyre and the house of Atreus. Agamemnon refers the matter to Calchas, who declares that Polyxena must die on the tomb as a bride and that Astyanax must be thrown from Troy's tower.","ideas":["Achilles' ghost halts the fleet","Agamemnon: power that is controlled lasts; he who does not forbid sin commands it","Pyrrhus: the victor may do what he wills","Calchas demands two deaths: Polyxena and Astyanax"],"terms":[{"t":"Aeacides","d":"Descendant of Aeacus, used for Achilles and Pyrrhus."},{"t":"Calchas","d":"Greek seer whose ruling decides the fate of the captives."}]},{"i":263,"j":287,"loc":"lines 371–523","title":"Nothing after death; Andromache hides Astyanax","summary":"The chorus asks whether the soul survives the body and answers that it does not: all things are swept away by time, the spirit vanishes like smoke, and the underworld is an idle tale, so that after death one lies where the unborn lie. Andromache enters with Astyanax and an old servant, saying that only her son keeps her from following Hector. She tells how Hector appeared to her in a dream, grieving, and ordered her to hide their son. Praising the boy's likeness to his father, she hides him in Hector's tomb on the old man's advice that the Greeks be told he died in the city's fall. As Ulysses approaches, she prays Hector to guard the child.","ideas":["Chorus: there is nothing after death, and death itself is nothing","Hector's ghost warns Andromache in a dream","The tomb as hiding-place: a refuge if the fates allow, a grave if not","The pretended death as a means of safety"],"terms":[]},{"i":288,"j":312,"loc":"lines 524–662","title":"Ulysses interrogates Andromache","summary":"Ulysses says he speaks for all the Greeks: Hector's son must die so that no new Hector can revive the war. Andromache first claims not to know where the boy is, defies threats of torture, and then swears he is already dead and buried. Ulysses, suspicious, watches her and notes that her fear is greater than her grief; he congratulates her that her son escaped death from the tower, then pretends the child has been found to test her trembling. Finally he announces that Calchas requires Hector's tomb to be razed and his ashes scattered on the sea. Andromache is torn between saving her husband's remains and her son, and decides to save the one the Greeks fear.","ideas":["Ulysses' argument: a hero's son grows into the father's likeness","Andromache's oath that her son is dead","Ulysses reads fear rather than grief in her face","The dilemma of son versus Hector's ashes"],"terms":[]},{"i":313,"j":341,"loc":"lines 663–813","title":"The tomb, surrender and farewell of Astyanax","summary":"Ulysses orders the tomb pulled down; Andromache resists like an Amazon or Maenad and calls on Hector, then realizes the falling mass will crush the hidden boy. She falls at Ulysses' knees, praying by his wife, father and son, and at his command calls Astyanax out, bidding him beg as a captive. She recalls how Hercules spared the boy Priam, and argues that a ruined Troy can threaten no one and a slave's lot is revenge enough. Ulysses replies that the Pelasgian mothers move him more and that Calchas refuses; Andromache denounces him as a contriver of fraud. Granted a brief delay, she laments the life her son will never have, sends kisses and a reproachful message to Hector, and the boy is led away.","ideas":["The razing of Hector's tomb forces Andromache to give up her son","Appeal to the example of Hercules sparing Priam","Ulysses shifts responsibility to Calchas","The mother's farewell and message to Hector"],"terms":[]},{"i":342,"j":366,"loc":"lines 814–1008","title":"Helen, Polyxena's doom and the allotment","summary":"The chorus wonders which Greek land each will be carried to, asking only to be spared Sparta, Argos, Mycenae and Ithaca. Helen admits privately that she must deceive Polyxena with a false marriage to Pyrrhus, and then urges her to dress as a bride. Andromache denounces Helen as the ruin of both peoples; Helen defends herself as a captive and a goddess's prize, then reveals that Polyxena is to be sacrificed at Achilles' tomb. Polyxena receives the news gladly and lets Helen dress her, while Hecuba faints and laments losing her last child. Helen announces the lots: Andromache to Pyrrhus, Cassandra to Agamemnon, Hecuba to Ulysses, whose voyage Hecuba curses; she then offers herself to Pyrrhus as he arrives to take Polyxena.","ideas":["Helen's deceit and her self-defence","Polyxena welcomes death as her bridal","The captives are assigned their masters","Hecuba's curse on Ulysses' homecoming"],"terms":[]},{"i":367,"j":383,"loc":"lines 1009–1179","title":"The deaths of Astyanax and Polyxena","summary":"The chorus reflects that grief is lighter when shared and that no one thinks himself unfortunate except by comparison, then imagines the moment when Troy's smoke will be the only sign of their homeland. A messenger reports both deaths. Crowds climbed trees, walls and even Hector's tomb to watch Ulysses lead Astyanax up Priam's tower, where the boy, unweeping, leaped down of his own will and was crushed. Then at Achilles' tomb, with Helen leading the wedding torches, Polyxena faced Pyrrhus' sword unafraid and fell as if to make the earth heavy on Achilles, while the mound drank her blood. Hecuba laments that death flees only her, and the messenger orders the captives to the ships.","ideas":["Shared grief and comparison of misfortunes","Astyanax's fall from the tower watched like a spectacle","Polyxena's courage at the sacrifice","The fleet departs once the two deaths are done"],"terms":[]},{"i":384,"j":400,"loc":"The Phoenician Women: introduction; lines 1–181","title":"Blind Oedipus longs to die","summary":"After the cast and argument (Oedipus wanders in exile with Antigone while his sons prepare to fight over Thebes), Oedipus bids Antigone leave him and seeks Cithaeron, where he was exposed, to die there; he believes he sees Laius attacking him. Antigone refuses to let go, offering to lead him wherever he wishes, even to his death, while urging him to master his sorrows. Oedipus marvels at so dutiful a child in his house, insists that the right to live or die is his own, and asks for a sword or a pyre. He imagines sitting on the Sphinx's rock to set out the riddle of his own kinship, and declares that death has a thousand doors; he would tear open his wounds to the brain. An editor's note marks a probable gap in the text.","ideas":["Oedipus seeks to die where he should have died as a child","Antigone's loyalty: without me you cannot perish, with me you can","Oedipus: anyone can take life, no one can take death","His own fate as a riddle worse than the Sphinx's"],"terms":[{"t":"Cithaeron","d":"Mountain near Thebes where the infant Oedipus was exposed."}]},{"i":401,"j":414,"loc":"lines 182–362","title":"Antigone persuades; Oedipus curses his sons","summary":"Antigone argues that true courage is to stand against misfortune, not to flee life, and that Oedipus is guiltless since the gods willed his acts. Oedipus answers that he flees himself and his own crimes, recounting how he was doomed before birth, killed his father and married his mother, and foreseeing that his sons will break their pact and destroy Thebes. When Antigone says he must live to restrain them, he consents to live for her sake. A messenger from Thebes (as the editor's note explains) begs him to stop the war; instead Oedipus urges his sons on to crimes worthy of their father, wishes brother to fight brother and their mother to be armed, and withdraws into the woods to listen for news. A further note says the following fragments may belong to another play.","ideas":["Antigone: manhood is to face great ills, not shrink from life","Oedipus guilty in his own eyes though innocent in intent","He agrees to live at his daughter's bidding","Asked to make peace, he curses his sons to war"],"terms":[]},{"i":415,"j":431,"loc":"lines 363–499","title":"Jocasta runs between the armies","summary":"Jocasta laments that she has borne guilty sons and must now pray for both: Polynices, exiled three years and now son-in-law of Adrastus, rightly seeks the throne but wrongly by war. A messenger reports the armies advancing with standards raised and begs her to stand between them; Antigone urges her on. Jocasta rushes off and parts the lines, which halt with swords poised. Kneeling between her sons, she bids them strike her womb first and asks for their hands. Polynices hesitates, fearing his brother's treachery and distrusting even his mother's pledge; Jocasta turns to Eteocles, who sheathes his sword.","ideas":["A mother cannot pray for one son without cursing the other","Jocasta places herself between the battle lines","Ignorance once made the family guilty; this would be the first crime done knowingly","Polynices' distrust; Eteocles lays down his arms"],"terms":[]},{"i":432,"j":447,"loc":"lines 500–664","title":"Jocasta pleads; Eteocles defends hated rule","summary":"Jocasta tells Polynices how she missed his wedding, that war was his bridal gift, and begs him by her womb, his sister and his father's eyes to spare his country, arguing that he is ravaging the very land he seeks to rule. Polynices asks whether he must remain an exile dependent on his wife's father while his brother enjoys his crime. Jocasta advises him to win other kingdoms in Asia with his allies, reminds him that war is uncertain and its guilt certain, and says his brother's punishment will be to reign. Eteocles replies that kingship is worth the price; he holds that the god joined hatred and sovereignty together, and that a ruler who would be loved rules with a nerveless hand. The text breaks off as he declares sovereignty well bought at any price.","ideas":["Destroying one's own country to rule it is self-defeating","Kingdoms won by crime are heavier than exile","Eteocles: who fears to be hated has no will to reign","The play survives incomplete"],"terms":[]},{"i":448,"j":468,"loc":"Phaedra: introduction; lines 1–128","title":"Hippolytus' hunt and Phaedra's lament","summary":"After the cast and argument (Theseus has been in the underworld four years with Pirithoüs, and Phaedra loves her stepson Hippolytus), Hippolytus at dawn sends his huntsmen across Attica, directs the hounds and nets, and prays to Diana, goddess of the hunt, before hurrying to the woods. Phaedra then laments that Crete gave her as hostage-wife to an enemy house, while Theseus faithlessly seeks to steal the queen of the dead. She describes a burning passion that has made her neglect her loom and worship and long to hunt in the forests. She recognizes in it her mother Pasiphaë's curse and blames Venus, who avenges herself on the race of the Sun.","ideas":["Hippolytus devoted to Diana and the hunt","Phaedra's resentment of Theseus' absence","Her love draws her toward the woods, as her mother's did","Venus' vengeance on the house of the Sun"],"terms":[{"t":"Pasiphaë","d":"Phaedra's mother, cursed with passion for a bull; mother of the Minotaur."}]},{"i":469,"j":498,"loc":"lines 129–273","title":"The Nurse argues against Phaedra's passion","summary":"The Nurse urges Phaedra to resist love at its outset, warns that impious crime is worse than monstrous passion, and says Minos, the Sun and Jove will see, and that conscience gives no peace even if the sin is hidden. Phaedra admits the truth of this but says passion has conquered reason and that Love is a mighty god even over gods. The Nurse replies that lust made love a god, and that such desire breeds in luxury and power rather than in humble homes; she warns of Theseus' return and Hippolytus' hatred of women. After quick exchanges, Phaedra resolves to die to save her honour. To prevent this, the Nurse offers to approach Hippolytus herself.","ideas":["Monstrous love may be blamed on fate, crime on character","Phaedra: with full knowledge she takes the worse path","Nurse: Love's divinity is a fiction of lust","Phaedra's resolve to die turns the Nurse to intrigue"],"terms":[]},{"i":499,"j":507,"loc":"lines 274–423","title":"Chorus on Love; Phaedra's sickness","summary":"The chorus sings of Venus' son, whose shafts reach every land and age and drive gods into lower forms: Phoebus herding cattle, Jove as swan and bull, the Moon leaving her chariot, Hercules in a Lydian gown at the spindle. Love rules the sea, birds and beasts too, and makes even stepmothers yield. The Nurse describes Phaedra's symptoms: fever, restlessness, sleeplessness, wasting, tears. Phaedra, carried out, throws off her finery and asks for an Amazon's dress, quiver and spear to go to the woods. The Nurse prays to Diana as three-formed Hecate to soften Hippolytus' heart, then sees him approaching and steels herself to the task.","ideas":["Love's power over every god and creature","Physical signs of Phaedra's hidden passion","Phaedra dresses as a huntress like Hippolytus' Amazon mother","The Nurse's prayer to Diana"],"terms":[{"t":"Hecate","d":"Three-formed goddess identified with Diana and the Moon, invoked by the Nurse."}]},{"i":508,"j":523,"loc":"lines 424–583","title":"Hippolytus praises the woodland life","summary":"The Nurse urges Hippolytus to relax his austerity, enjoy youth and Venus while life speeds by, and follow nature, since love renews the human race and the world would be empty without it. Hippolytus answers with a long praise of life in the woods: free from greed, flattery, fear and luxury, close to the life of the first age, before lust for gain, wrath and power brought war and kin-murder. He names woman as the leader of all wickedness, with Medea as proof. When the Nurse asks why the crime of a few should be blamed on all, he declares that he hates all women. The Nurse cites his Amazon mother as proof that love tames the stubborn; he answers that hating women is his consolation for losing her, and Phaedra is seen approaching.","ideas":["Nurse: follow nature as life's guide; love sustains the race","Hippolytus: the woodland life as innocence and freedom","Golden age and the decline into violence","His sweeping hatred of women"],"terms":[{"t":"primal age","d":"Hippolytus' first age of humanity, without property, walls or war."}]},{"i":524,"j":555,"loc":"lines 585–735","title":"Phaedra's declaration and the Nurse's accusation","summary":"Phaedra faints and is raised by Hippolytus; resolving to speak boldly, she asks for privacy. She hesitates, begs him not to call her mother but sister or slave, offers him the sceptre and asks pity for her widowhood; he promises to care for her until Theseus returns. She then confesses a burning love, saying she loves in him the young Theseus who came to Crete. Hippolytus calls on Jove to strike, denounces her as worse than Pasiphaë, and seizes her hair to kill her with his sword; she welcomes death at his hands. He throws away the polluted sword and flees to the woods. The Nurse turns the crime on him, crying to Athens that Hippolytus attacked the queen and keeping the sword as proof.","ideas":["Phaedra's gradual approach from slave to lover","She loves Theseus' youthful looks in his son","Hippolytus' horror and flight, leaving his sword","Nurse: crime must be concealed by crime"],"terms":[]},{"i":556,"j":599,"loc":"lines 736–958","title":"Theseus returns and curses his son","summary":"The chorus describes Hippolytus' flight, praises his beauty above Bacchus', warns that beauty is fleeting and dangerous even in the wild, and prays that his beauty go unpunished; it then notes Phaedra preparing false charges. Theseus arrives, pale and spent, freed from the underworld by Hercules. The Nurse says Phaedra is bent on death; Phaedra refuses to tell him why until he threatens to torture the Nurse. She then claims she was raped and shows Hippolytus' sword. Theseus denounces his son's Amazon blood and feigned austerity, swears to pursue him anywhere, and uses the last of Neptune's three promised prayers to ask for Hippolytus' death.","ideas":["Chorus: beauty is a brief and doubtful gift","Theseus' return from the underworld","Phaedra's false accusation with the sword as evidence","Theseus' curse through Neptune's boon"],"terms":[{"t":"Neptune's three prayers","d":"Boon granted to Theseus by his father, the sea-god, which he uses against his son."}]},{"i":600,"j":626,"loc":"lines 959–1155","title":"The bull from the sea and Hippolytus' death","summary":"The chorus asks why the ruler of the ordered heavens is indifferent to men and complains that blind Fortune rewards the wicked. A messenger reports that as Hippolytus drove his chariot along the shore, a wave rose without wind and cast up a huge sea-bull; all fled but Hippolytus, who held his horses and defied it, saying his father's task was conquering bulls. The horses bolted, he was thrown and entangled in the reins, dragged over rocks, impaled on a charred stake and torn apart, and his servants are gathering his scattered body. Theseus weeps, saying he weeps not for the loss but for having killed. The chorus observes that lightning strikes the heights while humble homes are spared, and that Fortune keeps faith with no one.","ideas":["Chorus doubts divine care for human justice","The sea-monster sent in answer to Theseus' prayer","The dragging and dismemberment of Hippolytus","Theseus' grief for the son he cursed"],"terms":[]},{"i":627,"j":644,"loc":"lines 1156–1280","title":"Phaedra's confession and Theseus' grief","summary":"Phaedra enters with a drawn sword, blames Theseus for bringing death to son and father, and mourns over Hippolytus' mangled face, vowing to follow him in death. She confesses to Athens and Theseus that she lied and that Hippolytus was chaste, then falls on her sword. Theseus calls on the underworld and the sea to take him, names himself his son's murderer, and asks for the punishments he saw below. The chorus bids him bury his son; Theseus tries to fit the torn limbs together, unable to tell some parts, and lays his last gifts on the body. He orders the royal pyre and the search for the missing parts, and commands that Phaedra be buried deep under heavy earth.","ideas":["Phaedra clears Hippolytus and kills herself","Theseus: guilt in heaven, hell and ocean","The father reassembling his son's body","Opposite rites for Hippolytus and Phaedra"],"terms":[]},{"i":645,"j":663,"loc":"Thyestes, lines 1–175","title":"Tantalus' ghost driven back to his house","summary":"After the cast list and an argument explaining the feud of Atreus and Thyestes over the throne, the stolen golden ram and Aërope, the ghost of Tantalus rises from the underworld, asking what new punishment awaits him. A Fury drives him to fill the house of Pelops with rage, kin-murder and a monstrous feast. Tantalus first refuses and begs to return to his torments, even warning against the crime, but the Fury's scourge and the burning hunger she wakes in him force him to obey; the land dries up at his presence. The Chorus of Mycenaean citizens prays that some god stop the recurring crimes of Tantalus' line, recalls Myrtilus and the son served to the gods, and describes Tantalus' endless hunger and thirst below.","ideas":["The ancestral curse as an infection the ghost spreads through the house","Tantalus would rather suffer punishment than become one","Drought and failing light as signs of coming crime","The chorus's catalogue of the family's earlier crimes and Tantalus' torment"],"terms":[{"t":"Furia (Fury)","d":"An avenging spirit of the underworld who here goads the ghost to stir up his descendants."},{"t":"Pelops' house","d":"The royal line of Argos and Mycenae descended from Tantalus, marked by inherited crime."}]},{"i":664,"j":710,"loc":"Thyestes, lines 176–403","title":"Atreus plots revenge; chorus on true kingship","summary":"Atreus rebukes himself for leaving his brother's wrongs unpunished and resolves on a crime that will surpass Thyestes' own. In debate with an attendant he holds that a king need not regard honour, piety or the people's disapproval, while the attendant urges restraint. Rejecting sword and fire as too little, he decides that Thyestes himself shall be the weapon: the father will devour his sons, after the Thracian feast of Procne. He will lure Thyestes with a false offer of shared rule, carried by his own sons, who are kept ignorant of the plan. The Chorus, wrongly thinking the brothers reconciled, sings that true kingship lies in freedom from fear and desire, not wealth or power, and prays for a quiet, obscure life.","ideas":["Atreus's view that kings stand above shame and right","Revenge must outdo the original wrong","The plan: a feigned reconciliation leading to a cannibal feast","The chorus: the king is one who fears and desires nothing"],"terms":[{"t":"pietas (piety)","d":"Dutiful regard for family and gods, which Atreus explicitly banishes from his house."},{"t":"rex (king)","d":"In the chorus, the person whose upright mind rules himself rather than others."}]},{"i":711,"j":753,"loc":"Thyestes, lines 404–622","title":"Thyestes returns and is welcomed","summary":"Thyestes arrives with his three sons, glad to see Argos yet seized by fear he cannot explain; he praises the safe, simple life of exile over the dangers of power, while his son Tantalus urges him on. Atreus, aside, gloats like a hound scenting its prey, then embraces his brother with a show of love. Thyestes confesses his guilt and offers his sons as pledges; he refuses the crown, but Atreus insists, sets it on his head, and goes off to make sacrifice. The Chorus marvels at the sudden peace, comparing it to a sea calming after a storm, then warns that no lot endures, that every power is subject to a greater, and that no one can promise himself tomorrow.","ideas":["Thyestes' instinctive dread and his praise of poverty","Atreus's hidden glee behind the embrace","The crown pressed on a reluctant Thyestes","The chorus on the fickleness of fortune"],"terms":[{"t":"Fortuna (fortune)","d":"Chance that raises and lowers men, a recurring theme of the choral odes."}]},{"i":754,"j":780,"loc":"Thyestes, lines 623–788","title":"Messenger describes the murder of the sons","summary":"A messenger arrives in horror and, pressed by the Chorus, describes the hidden grove deep in Pelops' palace, a place of dark trees, a Styx-like spring, ghosts and oracles. There Atreus, acting as his own priest with full sacrificial ritual, slaughtered Thyestes' sons at the altar while portents shook the house: Tantalus first, then Plisthenes, then the third boy. He read the fates in their entrails, cut up the bodies, and boiled and roasted the flesh, sparing only heads and hands; the fire burned grudgingly and the smoke settled on the household gods. Thyestes now feasts on his sons unknowing, and the sun has turned back, but the messenger says the crime must come out.","ideas":["The ancient grove where the Tantalids are crowned","Murder performed as a perverted sacrifice","Portents: shaking palace, weeping statues, wine turned to blood","The sun flees the crime in mid-day"],"terms":[{"t":"haruspicy","d":"Reading the fates from victims' entrails, which Atreus performs on the boys' organs."}]},{"i":781,"j":819,"loc":"Thyestes, lines 789–1112","title":"Darkness, the revelation, and the brothers' curses","summary":"The Chorus asks why the sun has vanished at midday and fears that the cosmos is collapsing into chaos, listing the zodiac signs that will fall. Atreus enters exulting, opens the banquet hall and watches the drunken Thyestes, who tries to cast off old griefs and sing but is overcome by causeless dread and tears. Atreus hands him a cup mixed with his sons' blood, then uncovers their heads and tells him he has eaten them. Thyestes calls on earth and Jove to destroy them and begs for burial of the remains; Atreus boasts of every detail, says he regrets only that the father was unwitting, and charges that Thyestes had planned the same. The play ends with Thyestes leaving Atreus to the gods and Atreus leaving him to his sons.","ideas":["Cosmic disorder as the response to the feast","Thyestes' unexplained foreboding amid false joy","Recognition: 'Dost recognize thy sons?' and the brother","Atreus's satisfaction that his sons are now proved his own"],"terms":[{"t":"chaos","d":"The formless state the chorus fears the world will fall back into."},{"t":"zodiac","d":"The band of constellations whose fall the chorus imagines, sign by sign."}]},{"i":820,"j":840,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 1–232","title":"Hercules claims heaven; captive maidens lament","summary":"After the cast list and argument, Hercules, having destroyed Oechalia and its king Eurytus, addresses Jupiter: he has cleared earth, sea and underworld of monsters and tyrants, and asks why heaven is still denied him. He lists his labours, complains that Juno has set his slain beasts among the stars, and sends Lichas ahead while he goes to sacrifice to Cenaean Jove. The Chorus of captive Oechalian maidens sings that one who can die easily is never wretched, mourns their city's ruin and coming slavery, and describes Hercules' invulnerable body. Iole laments her father and brother, wishes to be turned into a mourning bird, and blames her beauty for her house's fall; the Chorus counsels bearing either lot with an even mind.","ideas":["Hercules' catalogue of labours as a claim to the stars","Juno's hatred and the monsters made constellations","The fall of Oechalia because Eurytus refused Iole","Iole's wish for transformation into a bird"],"terms":[{"t":"Alcides","d":"Name for Hercules as descendant of Alcaeus, used throughout the translation."}]},{"i":841,"j":862,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 233–435","title":"Deïanira's jealous rage at Trachin","summary":"At Trachin the Nurse describes Deïanira's frenzy on seeing the beautiful captive Iole. Deïanira prays Juno to send a monster against Hercules or make her own heart one, and vows that the day which ends her marriage will end his life. The Nurse warns that all Greece and Jove would avenge him and argues that his love for a fallen princess will pass, as his loves for Auge, the Thespiades and Omphale did. Deïanira answers that her own beauty is fading with age and childbearing while Iole's shines through misfortune, and that Hercules wanders the world chiefly for women, sacking cities when fathers refuse him. She resolves to strike first.","ideas":["The wife's wrath pictured as a worse pest than the Hydra","The Nurse's list of Hercules' past, passing loves","Deïanira's fear of aging and of losing so great a husband","Her charge that Hercules' heroism is driven by lust"],"terms":[]},{"i":863,"j":908,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 436–699","title":"The Nessus charm and the poisoned robe","summary":"Deïanira declares she will kill Hercules, by guile if not by sword, though she still loves him. The Nurse proposes magic to win him back, and Deïanira reveals her secret: the dying centaur Nessus, shot by Hercules for trying to carry her off at the river Evenus, gave her his poisoned blood as a love-charm to be used if a rival appeared, kept from light. She prays to Cupid to make Hercules love his wife, and sends the anointed robe by Lichas with instructions for a sacrifice. The Chorus of Aetolian women pledges loyalty, then sings that kings have few true friends, that greed is never filled, that the poor sleep safely, and that the middle course is safe, citing Phaëthon and Icarus.","ideas":["The story of Nessus at the Evenus","The 'love-charm' that must not see sunlight","The robe sent by Lichas","Choral praise of modest fortune and the middle way"],"terms":[{"t":"Nessus","d":"The centaur whose blood, poisoned by Hercules' arrow, Deïanira believes is a love-charm."}]},{"i":909,"j":931,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 700–841","title":"The charm revealed; Hyllus reports the agony","summary":"Deïanira returns in terror: suspecting Nessus, she tested the blood-soaked wool in the sun, and it burst into flame and dissolved, making even the ground foam. Her son Hyllus rushes in, telling her to flee, and reports that Hercules is dying. At the shrine of Cenaean Jove on Euboea, Hercules put on the robe and prayed that peace was now complete, then cried out in pain. He seized Lichas and hurled him to his death, then tore at the robe, which had fused with his flesh, ripping his own limbs. He is now being shipped home unconscious.","ideas":["The test in sunlight exposes the poison","Hercules' prayer of completed labours at the altar","The killing of Lichas","The robe grown into his body"],"terms":[]},{"i":932,"j":973,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 842–1130","title":"Deïanira's remorse; Orpheus and universal death","summary":"Deïanira condemns herself as the destroyer of the world's protector and seeks death, asking Jove's bolt or a fall from Oeta. The Nurse argues that one who errs without intent is not guilty and recalls that Hercules forgave himself for killing Megara and his sons; Deïanira insists that death alone can make the deceived innocent. She begs Hyllus to kill her, then sees the Furies and the punished souls, feels the world rising against her, and rushes out; Hyllus follows to prevent her. The Chorus recalls Orpheus, whose song moved nature and the underworld but who lost Eurydice, and who taught that nothing is made for endless life. Hercules' fall confirms it; they imagine the end of the cosmos.","ideas":["Error versus crime: the Nurse's case for Deïanira's innocence","Deïanira's vision of the Furies","Orpheus' teaching that all that is born must die","The coming collapse of heaven, earth and Tartarus"],"terms":[{"t":"error","d":"A wrong done through mistaken judgment; the Nurse and Hyllus say it is not counted as crime."},{"t":"Megaera","d":"One of the Furies, whom Deïanira sees pursuing her with a torch."}]},{"i":974,"j":985,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 1131–1336","title":"Hercules' agony and prayers for death","summary":"Hercules enters in torment, asking the sun to turn back and fearing that giants will rise against heaven once he is gone. He laments that he, who escaped the Styx, dies without an enemy and, worse, at a woman's hand. He describes the plague eating his skin, flesh, marrow and bones, contrasts his ruined body with the arms that did his labours, and challenges the hidden pest to fight openly. Ashamed to find himself weeping for the first time, he begs Jupiter for a thunderbolt, asks Mars, Pallas and even Juno to strike him, and finally calls on the cities of earth to stone him in return for his services.","ideas":["Shame at an inglorious death without a monster slain","The poison consuming his body inward","The first tears of Hercules","Prayers to every power for a death"],"terms":[]},{"i":986,"j":1017,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 1337–1517","title":"Alcmena arrives; the oracle fulfilled","summary":"Alcmena finds her son reduced to a shadow; Hercules tells her a woman's poisoned robe has done it and describes the Hydra raging inside him, while she urges him to master the pain, suggesting overwork, not poison, is to blame. He collapses, wakes in a delirium of being received into heaven, then returns to his senses and rages against Deïanira. Hyllus reports that she has killed herself and explains that Nessus contrived the snare. Hercules recognises an oracle that he would fall by one he had slain, accepts his fate, and orders a pyre on Oeta, to be lit by Philoctetes. He asks Hyllus to marry Iole and comforts Alcmena that his deeds have earned him Jove as father.","ideas":["Alcmena's grief at her son's ruined body","Delirium: a glimpse of heaven opening","Hyllus clears his mother by blaming Nessus","The oracle and the choice of a glorious death on the pyre"],"terms":[{"t":"oracle","d":"The prophecy from Dodona's oak and Delphi that Hercules would die by the hand of one he had slain."}]},{"i":1018,"j":1037,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 1518–1757","title":"Lament for Hercules; Philoctetes tells of the pyre","summary":"The Chorus mourns Hercules as the world's protector, asks who will defend it from new monsters, foresees him judging among the dead or shining among the stars, and prays Jove to spare the world tyrants and beasts. Philoctetes arrives and describes the felling of Oeta's forest for the pyre. Hercules gave him his bow and arrows, had the club and lion's skin burned with him, rebuked Alcmena's wailing, and prayed Jove to receive him among the stars. Lying calmly on the pyre, he urged the hesitant Philoctetes to light it, then followed the shrinking flames, never groaning or closing his eyes, and went into the hottest fire.","ideas":["The world left without a champion","The bow passed to Philoctetes","Hercules' prayer from the pyre","Fire as the last of his conquered toils"],"terms":[{"t":"Philoctetes","d":"Son of Poeas, Hercules' friend, who lights the pyre and inherits his bow."}]},{"i":1038,"j":1057,"loc":"Hercules on Oeta, lines 1758–1996","title":"Alcmena's mourning and Hercules' apotheosis","summary":"Alcmena enters carrying the urn of her son's ashes, marvelling that so great a body fits in it. She fears for herself, hated by the kings and kin of those he killed and by Juno, wonders where to flee, and declares the whole world his tomb. Hyllus tells her not to mourn a man whose valour conquered fate, but she sings a formal lament calling on Crete, Arcadia, Thrace, the gods and every land he freed to weep. The voice of Hercules then speaks from the sky: his mortal part from her has burned, his father's part has risen to heaven, and Eurystheus will pay. Alcmena goes to proclaim the new god at Thebes, and the Chorus sings that valour is never borne to the shades.","ideas":["The urn: the hero shrunk to dust","Alcmena's fear of Hercules' enemies","The voice from heaven and the division of mortal and divine parts","Valour opens the path to heaven"],"terms":[{"t":"apotheosis","d":"Being made a god; Hercules' immortal part ascends to the stars after the pyre."},{"t":"virtus (valour)","d":"Courage and excellence, which the closing chorus says carries the brave to heaven."}]},{"i":1058,"j":1096,"loc":"Agamemnon, lines 1–225","title":"Thyestes' ghost; Clytemnestra weighs murder","summary":"After the cast list and an argument summarising the feud and Clytemnestra's grievances, the ghost of Thyestes rises at Mycenae, recalls eating his sons and fathering Aegisthus on his own daughter, and announces that Agamemnon is near, to be slain by his wife; he urges Aegisthus on. The Chorus of Argive women sings that Fortune sets kings on dangerous heights and that great things fall by their own weight, praising a modest lot. Clytemnestra tells herself the honest way is closed and debates with her Nurse, recalling the sacrifice of her daughter at Aulis and Agamemnon's love of Chryseis, Briseis and now Cassandra. The Nurse warns that the conqueror of Asia cannot be slain by stealth and that Greece would avenge him.","ideas":["Thyestes' crimes and the prophecy fulfilled through Aegisthus","Fortune topples the too exalted","Iphigenia's sacrifice as Clytemnestra's grievance","The Nurse's warning against killing the victor"],"terms":[{"t":"Fortuna (fortune)","d":"The power the chorus says lifts kings only to bring them low."}]},{"i":1097,"j":1122,"loc":"Agamemnon, lines 226–411","title":"Aegisthus wins Clytemnestra; hymn to the gods","summary":"Aegisthus resolves to face his fate and finds Clytemnestra wavering: she now wishes to return to her duty and says one who repents is nearly innocent. He argues that Agamemnon, swollen by victory, will bring Cassandra as a rival and that kings punish others for what they allow themselves. She retorts by scorning his incestuous birth and orders him to leave, but when he offers to go or die she relents, saying partners in crime owe faith to one another, and takes him in to plan. The Chorus sings a festal hymn of thanksgiving to Phoebus, Juno, Pallas, Trivia and Jove for the victory at Troy.","ideas":["Clytemnestra's brief impulse to repent","Aegisthus plays on her jealousy and fear","Their quarrel over his birth","The chorus's victory hymn amid the plot"],"terms":[]},{"i":1123,"j":1141,"loc":"Agamemnon, lines 392a–586","title":"Eurybates tells of the storm at sea","summary":"The herald Eurybates announces Agamemnon's return, and Clytemnestra asks after Menelaus and Helen. He relates the fleet's joyful departure from Troy, then a sudden night storm in which all the winds fought, ships crashed together and sank, and Greeks and Trojans prayed alike, envying those who died at Troy. Pallas struck Ajax with Jove's lightning; he survived, boasted on a rock that he had conquered flood and flame, and was drowned when Neptune broke the rock. Then Nauplius, father of Palamedes, lured the survivors onto the reefs of Caphereus with a false beacon. Dawn revealed the ruin, and the herald prays that the gods be appeased.","ideas":["The serene start of the voyage home","The storm as cosmic chaos at sea","The defiance and death of Ajax","Nauplius' false beacon at Caphereus"],"terms":[{"t":"Caphereus","d":"A rocky headland of Euboea where the Greek fleet was wrecked by a false light."}]},{"i":1142,"j":1156,"loc":"Agamemnon, lines 586–781","title":"Trojan captives and Cassandra's vision","summary":"Cassandra arrives with the captive Trojan women, who sing that death is a peaceful haven for the wretched and lament the fall of Troy through the wooden horse and the death of Priam. Cassandra refuses to share her grief and casts off her priestly fillets, saying her woes have exhausted Fortune. Seized by the god against her will, she sees Paris judging the goddesses, a woman with an axe, and a lion brought down by his lioness, and she welcomes her dead kin and the underworld. She foresees that the ferry will carry royal souls, victor and vanquished, this day, then collapses as Agamemnon approaches with his wife.","ideas":["Death as release, which the captives praise","Troy's fall by treachery, not by arms","Cassandra's prophetic seizure","The lion and lioness: a veiled image of the murder"],"terms":[{"t":"vates (prophetess)","d":"Cassandra, priestess of Phoebus, whose true prophecies go unheeded."}]},{"i":1157,"j":1224,"loc":"Agamemnon, lines 782–1012","title":"Agamemnon's murder and Electra's defiance","summary":"Agamemnon greets his homeland and tells Cassandra that this is a festal day; she answers with ominous one-line replies, and he goes in to sacrifice. The Chorus praises Argos for Hercules and his labours. Cassandra, alone, sees the murder as it happens within: at the feast Clytemnestra gives Agamemnon a robe with no outlet for hands or head, Aegisthus wounds him weakly, and she kills him with an axe. Electra sends her brother Orestes off with Strophius of Phocis and Pylades, then defies her mother, refusing to reveal where he is hidden. Aegisthus orders her imprisoned rather than killed, and Clytemnestra sends Cassandra to death; Cassandra goes gladly and foretells madness for them too.","ideas":["Cassandra's double-edged exchange with Agamemnon","The murder in the entangling robe","Orestes rescued by Strophius","Electra's defiance and Cassandra's last prophecy"],"terms":[{"t":"Tyndaris","d":"Clytemnestra as daughter of Tyndareus and Helen's sister."}]},{"i":1225,"j":1249,"loc":"Oedipus, front matter and lines 1–201","title":"Oedipus laments the plague on Thebes","summary":"After the cast list and an argument recounting the oracle given to Laïus, the exposed infant, and Oedipus's rise to the throne by solving the Sphinx's riddle, Oedipus opens the play at a gloomy dawn. He reflects that royalty is a deceitful good, recalls fleeing Corinth to escape the oracle that he would kill his father and wed his mother, and fears that the plague sparing him alone marks him for some worse fate. Jocasta urges him to stand firm against Fortune; he insists he is no coward, recalling his victory over the Sphinx. The chorus of Theban elders then describes the plague in detail: dying flocks and herds, withered crops, the dead crowding the seven gates, portents from the underworld, and the symptoms of the disease.","ideas":["Kingship exposes its holder to fate as peaks catch the wind","Oedipus fled Corinth out of distrust of himself, to keep nature's laws","The plague spares only the king, who suspects he has made heaven pestilent","The chorus catalogues the plague's ravages on beasts, land and people"],"terms":[{"t":"Sphinx","d":"The winged monster whose riddle Oedipus solved, winning the throne and Jocasta's hand."},{"t":"Delphic oracle","d":"Apollo's prophecy that Oedipus would kill his father and share his mother's bed."}]},{"i":1250,"j":1297,"loc":"Oedipus, lines 202–402","title":"Creon's oracle and Tiresias's sacrifice","summary":"Creon returns from Delphi and, after hesitating, reports the god's verse reply: the plague will end when the stained exile who killed King Laïus leaves Thebes. Oedipus pronounces a solemn curse on the unknown murderer, praying that he suffer exile, shameful marriage and parricide, and Creon describes how Laïus was killed by robbers at a three-forked road. The blind prophet Tiresias arrives with his daughter Manto, who reports the omens of the sacrifice: a flame that splits in two, wine turning to blood, a bull that shrinks from the sun, diseased and displaced entrails, and an unnatural foetus. Since the signs cannot reveal the name, Tiresias resolves to summon Laïus's ghost, and Oedipus sends Creon to the rite.","ideas":["The oracle demands the banishment of Laïus's killer","Oedipus unknowingly curses himself in his imprecation","The sacrifice yields monstrous omens of reversed nature","Necromancy is chosen as the way to learn the murderer's name"],"terms":[{"t":"extispicy","d":"Divination by inspecting the entrails of sacrificed animals, which Manto performs and describes."},{"t":"Manto","d":"Tiresias's daughter, who guides her blind father and reports the visible signs of the rite."}]},{"i":1298,"j":1348,"loc":"Oedipus, lines 403–708","title":"Hymn to Bacchus and the raising of Laïus","summary":"The chorus sings a long hymn to Bacchus, recounting his youth disguised as a girl, his eastern conquests, the death of Pentheus, the pirates turned into dolphins, and his marriage to the deserted maiden of Naxos. Creon returns and, under threat, describes the necromancy in a dark grove: Tiresias's rites, the earth splitting open, the Furies and the ghosts of Thebes, and at last Laïus himself. The ghost names the king as the bloodstained killer who has married his mother, and demands his exile. Oedipus objects that Polybus and Merope are alive, accuses Creon and Tiresias of plotting for the throne, and has Creon imprisoned after an exchange on fear and kingship.","ideas":["Bacchus's triumphs and transformations celebrated by Thebes","The ghost of Laïus reveals parricide and incest as the cause of the plague","Oedipus suspects a conspiracy and imprisons Creon","Debate on whether fear guards kingdoms or recoils on tyrants"],"terms":[{"t":"necromancy","d":"The summoning of the dead to speak, performed here by Tiresias to raise Laïus."},{"t":"Erinys","d":"A Fury; Laïus threatens to bring her to Oedipus's marriage as bridesmaid."}]},{"i":1349,"j":1418,"loc":"Oedipus, lines 709–881","title":"Memory, the Corinthian messenger, and the truth","summary":"The chorus traces Thebes's troubles back to Cadmus's founding: the serpent, the sown men who killed each other, and Actaeon's transformation. Oedipus recalls killing an arrogant old man at the three-forked road, and Jocasta's answers on Laïus's age, escort and the time confirm his fear. An old man from Corinth announces that Polybus has died peacefully, then reveals that Merope was not Oedipus's mother: he himself received the infant with pierced feet from a shepherd on Cithaeron. Despite Jocasta's warning, Oedipus summons the royal herdsman Phorbas, who under threat of torture admits the child was born of the king's wife. Oedipus cries out for the earth to swallow him and rushes into the palace.","ideas":["The house of Cadmus has been cursed since its founding","Details of the killing at the crossroads match Laïus's death","The Corinthian's news frees Oedipus of parricide only to expose his birth","Phorbas's forced confession completes the recognition"],"terms":[{"t":"Cithaeron","d":"The mountain where the infant Oedipus was handed from Phorbas to the Corinthian herdsman."},{"t":"Oedipus (name)","d":"Explained here as taken from his swollen, pierced feet."}]},{"i":1419,"j":1443,"loc":"Oedipus, lines 882–1061","title":"The blinding of Oedipus and Jocasta's death","summary":"The chorus praises the middle course, citing Icarus's fall and Daedalus's safer flight. A messenger reports how Oedipus, raging like a lion, rejected a quick death by the sword as too small a payment and tore out his own eyes, then thanked the gods for a night fit for his marriage. The chorus declares that all things move by fixed fate. Oedipus enters blind and content with the darkness; Jocasta seeks to speak to him as son, and he begs her to spare him. She blames fate, then takes his sword and stabs herself in the womb. Oedipus calls himself twice a parricide and leaves Thebes, taking the plague with him.","ideas":["Excess beyond allotted bounds is unsafe, as Icarus shows","Oedipus chooses blindness as a lasting death","Jocasta kills herself through the womb that bore husband and sons","Oedipus departs, drawing the pestilence after him"],"terms":[{"t":"Lachesis","d":"The Fate whose distaff fixes each life's course, invoked by the chorus."},{"t":"middle course","d":"The chorus's ideal of moderation, illustrated by Daedalus and Icarus."}]},{"i":1444,"j":1460,"loc":"Medea, front matter and lines 1–115","title":"Medea's invocation and the wedding hymn","summary":"The cast list and argument set out the Argonaut story, Medea's crimes for Jason, their exile in Corinth, and Jason's coming marriage to Creon's daughter Creusa. Medea calls on the gods of marriage, Hecate, the underworld and the Furies to destroy the bride, her father and the royal house, and prays that Jason live on as a homeless wanderer. She resolves that greater crimes become her now that she is a mother, and that a home gained by crime must be left by crime. The chorus of Corinthians then sings the wedding hymn for Jason and Creusa, praising the bride's beauty and rejoicing that Jason is freed from a barbarian wife.","ideas":["Medea invokes heaven and hell for vengeance on the new marriage","Her worst wish for Jason is that he should live","She vows to surpass her earlier crimes","The chorus celebrates Jason's release from the 'unruly' Colchian wife"],"terms":[{"t":"epithalamium","d":"A wedding song, here sung by the chorus for Jason and Creusa."},{"t":"Hecate","d":"The three-formed goddess of night and magic, Medea's chief patron."}]},{"i":1461,"j":1524,"loc":"Medea, lines 116–379","title":"Medea faces the Nurse and Creon","summary":"Hearing the wedding hymn, Medea recalls her crimes done for love and fixes the blame on Creon. Her Nurse urges silent patience, but Medea answers in short defiant lines that Medea alone remains to her. Creon orders her into exile; she argues that her only crime was saving the Argonauts, and that Jason shares her guilt. Creon holds Jason innocent but finally grants her one day's delay to take leave of her sons. The chorus then sings of the first bold ship, the innocent age before seafaring, the Argo's perils, and the prize won, the fleece and Medea, ending with a prophecy of new worlds beyond Thule.","ideas":["Medea claims her crimes were never done for herself","The stichomythia with the Nurse ends in 'Medea will I be'","Creon fears her and grants one fatal day","The chorus condemns the Argo for breaking nature's boundaries"],"terms":[{"t":"stichomythia","d":"Rapid exchange of single lines, used in Medea's dialogue with the Nurse."},{"t":"Argo","d":"The first ship, which carried the Argonauts to Colchis and brought Medea back."},{"t":"Thule","d":"The farthest known land, which the chorus foresees will one day cease to be the limit."}]},{"i":1525,"j":1575,"loc":"Medea, lines 380–567","title":"Medea and Jason confront each other","summary":"The Nurse describes Medea rushing about like a maenad, and Medea vows that her rage will match her love and never cease. Jason enters, explaining that he broke faith only to save his children from death. Medea reminds him of the bull, the sown men, the dragon, her brother and Pelias, and begs him to give back what she gave up for him. Jason pleads Creon's threat and Acastus's pursuit; Medea urges him to flee with her, and calls on Jupiter's bolt to strike whichever of them is guilty. When Jason refuses to let the children go with her, saying they are his reason for living, she sees where to wound him and feigns reconciliation.","ideas":["Jason claims love for his sons overcame his faith","Medea lists her services and asks for her dowry back","'Who profits by a sin has done the sin'","Jason's love for his sons reveals his weak point"],"terms":[{"t":"Acastus","d":"Pelias's son and king of Thessaly, who demands Jason and Medea for punishment."},{"t":"Creusa","d":"Creon's daughter, Jason's new bride."}]},{"i":1576,"j":1602,"loc":"Medea, lines 568–878","title":"The poisoned gifts and Medea's incantation","summary":"Medea tells the Nurse she will send the robe, necklace and golden band from the Sun's house to Creusa, steeped in poison. The chorus sings that nothing burns like a wife robbed of love, then lists the dreadful ends of the Argonauts, from Tiphys and Orpheus to Hercules and Pelias, praying that Jason be spared. The Nurse describes Medea gathering serpents and deadly herbs from across the world. Medea's incantation calls on the dead and on Hecate, recounts her power over nature, offers relics such as Nessus's blood, draws her own blood, and charges the gifts with Promethean fire. She sends her sons with the gifts; the chorus fears what her joined anger and love will bring.","ideas":["Heirlooms from the Sun become instruments of murder","The Argonauts pay for violating the sea's laws","Medea's magic claims power over seas, seasons and stars","Her sons carry the poisoned gifts to the bride"],"terms":[{"t":"Tiphys","d":"The Argo's helmsman, first named among the Argonauts punished by the sea."},{"t":"Trivia / Dictynna","d":"Names of Hecate–Diana under which Medea invokes the moon goddess."}]},{"i":1603,"j":1634,"loc":"Medea, lines 879–1027","title":"Burning palace and the murder of the sons","summary":"A messenger reports that Creusa and Creon lie in mingled ashes and that water only feeds the fire consuming the palace. Medea refuses to flee and, judging Jason's loss of a wife too small a vengeance, resolves to punish him through their children. She wavers between mother and wronged wife, sees the Furies and her brother's ghost, and kills the first son as an offering to him. From the rooftop she taunts Jason, who begs for the second boy; she kills him before Jason's eyes, throws down the bodies, and departs in a serpent-drawn chariot. Jason calls after her that there are no gods.","ideas":["The poisoned gifts destroy Creusa, Creon and the palace","Medea's inner struggle between wrath and love","The killings are staged for Jason to witness","The play ends with Jason denying the gods"],"terms":[{"t":"Furies (Megaera)","d":"Avenging goddesses whom Medea imagines pursuing her with her brother's ghost."}]},{"i":1635,"j":1668,"loc":"Octavia, Introduction and historical summary","title":"Introduction: Roman historical drama and Nero's family","summary":"The translator's introduction explains that Roman tragedy mostly imitated Greek models, and lists the few known fabulae praetextae on Roman subjects, from Naevius's Clastidium to Accius's Brutus and Decius and later plays. These were mostly occasional pieces; the Octavia is the only complete surviving example. Its authorship is unknown: tradition assigns it to Seneca, but most critics reject this because it describes Nero's death, which came after Seneca's. A summary of imperial history follows: Messalina's fall, Agrippina's marriage to Claudius and her plots for Nero, the poisonings of Claudius and Britannicus, the murder of Agrippina, and Octavia's divorce, exile and death.","ideas":["Roman drama was overwhelmingly modelled on Greek tragedy","The Octavia is the only complete Roman historical play","Seneca's authorship is doubted because of the prophecy of Nero's end","Background: Agrippina's crimes and Poppaea's rise"],"terms":[{"t":"fabula praetexta","d":"Roman historical drama, named for the purple-bordered toga of magistrates."},{"t":"Pandataria","d":"The island to which Octavia was banished in 62 A.D. and where she was killed."}]},{"i":1669,"j":1711,"loc":"Octavia, opening scenes","title":"Octavia and her Nurse lament the house","summary":"Octavia greets the dawn with grief for her murdered mother and her father Claudius, blaming her stepmother Agrippina. Her Nurse reflects on the fall of the house of Claudius and fears some desperate deed. In dialogue, Octavia describes her hatred for Nero, her dreams of Britannicus's murder, and her fear of Poppaea, who demands her death. The Nurse recounts Agrippina's crimes and the poisoning of Britannicus, then urges Octavia to win Nero by compliance, as Juno outlasted Jupiter's loves. Octavia refuses, calls on Jupiter to strike Nero, and traces the gods' wrath on her house back to Messalina's adulterous marriage.","ideas":["Octavia mourns mother, father and brother","Nero hates her and loves the mistress Poppaea","The Nurse counsels patience, citing Juno","Octavia prays for Jupiter's thunderbolt against Nero"],"terms":[{"t":"Britannicus","d":"Octavia's brother, poisoned by Nero, whose ghost haunts her dreams."},{"t":"Agrippina","d":"Nero's mother and Octavia's stepmother, blamed for Claudius's death."}]},{"i":1712,"j":1782,"loc":"Octavia, first chorus and Seneca–Nero scene","title":"Agrippina's shipwreck and Seneca's counsel to Nero","summary":"The chorus of Romans laments the rumour of a new marriage, recalls the old Romans who avenged Virginia and Lucretia, and narrates Nero's collapsing ship, Agrippina's swim to safety, and her murder by the sword. Seneca, alone, regrets leaving his Corsican exile and traces the decline of humankind from the golden age to an age of crime and luxury. Nero orders the heads of Plautus and Sulla; in rapid exchanges Seneca urges clemency, loyalty and love over fear, while Nero answers that the sword protects the prince. Nero cites Augustus's proscriptions as precedent, rejects Octavia, and over Seneca's objections on love and public feeling sets the wedding with Poppaea for the next day.","ideas":["Agrippina's death by her son's design","Seneca's account of the ages of mankind and Astraea's flight","Clemency versus fear as the basis of rule","Nero fixes his marriage to Poppaea"],"terms":[{"t":"Astraea","d":"The virgin Justice who ruled the golden age and fled the earth as men grew wicked."},{"t":"clementia (clemency)","d":"Mercy in a ruler, which Seneca calls the sovereign cure for fear."}]},{"i":1783,"j":1807,"loc":"Octavia, Agrippina's ghost to the people's revolt","title":"Agrippina's ghost, Poppaea's dream, the people rise","summary":"Agrippina's ghost arrives from Tartarus with a Stygian torch for the wedding, recalls her murder, and foretells Nero's abandoned and violent end. Octavia, now divorced, asks the chorus to hold back their tears and leaves the palace. Poppaea emerges shaken by a dream of mourning matrons, Agrippina's bloody torch, and Nero killing her former husband Crispinus; her Nurse interprets it favourably, and Poppaea goes to sacrifice. A chorus praises Poppaea's beauty. A messenger reports that the people, loving Octavia, are tearing down Poppaea's statues and threatening the palace with fire; the chorus warns that Cupid's power will crush them.","ideas":["The murdered mother prophesies her son's downfall","Octavia is driven from the palace on the wedding day","Poppaea's ominous dream and its hopeful reading","Popular revolt in Octavia's favour"],"terms":[{"t":"Poppaea","d":"Nero's mistress, now his wife, whose statues the crowd overturns."}]},{"i":1808,"j":1844,"loc":"Octavia, closing scenes","title":"Nero condemns Octavia to exile and death","summary":"Nero rages that the revolt has been too gently suppressed, threatens to burn Rome, and blames Octavia for stirring the people. In a sharp exchange the Prefect resists killing a woman, but Nero orders her shipped to a distant shore and executed. The chorus reflects on how popular favour has destroyed the Gracchi and Livius Drusus. Octavia is dragged to the ship that once carried Agrippina; she declares there are no gods and longs for a nightingale's wings. The chorus consoles her with the fates of earlier women of her house, and she sails for Pandataria as the chorus prays she be carried off like Iphigenia, since Rome delights in her children's blood.","ideas":["Nero treats the people's love for Octavia as her crime","Popular favour is fatal to those it exalts","Octavia boards the same deadly ship as Agrippina","The play ends with Rome judged crueller than the Taurians"],"terms":[{"t":"Gracchi","d":"Roman reformers cited by the chorus as destroyed by the people's excessive favour."}]}]});